<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>computational-methods-or-mechanics</title></head><body><h1>computational-methods-or-mechanics</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Numerical computation: roots of algebraic and transcenden…</li><li>Mechanics â€” Statics and Dynamics</li><li>Numerical integration: Trapezoidal rule and Simpson's rule</li><li>Mechanics (optional): Statics — forces and resultant forc…</li><li>Mechanics (optional): Dynamics — motion of particle in a …</li></ul><h2>Numerical computation: roots of algebraic and transcenden…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Numerical computation: roots of algebraic and transcenden… — Computational Methods or Mechanics, Mathematics (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: numerical computation, roots of algebraic, transcendental equations, bisection · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** 1st law (inertia): a body stays in uniform motion unless acted on by a net external force.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** 2nd law: net force = rate of change of momentum = m·a (for constant m).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** 3rd law: forces are mutual, equal and opposite, acting on different bodies (never cancel on the same body).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Fact.** Impulse = ∫F dt = Δp; F = ma gives F = m·(Δv/Δt).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Roots of algebraic.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Transcendental equations.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Bisection.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Newton-Raphson method.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Numerical computation</td></tr><tr><td>• Roots of algebraic</td></tr><tr><td>• Transcendental equations</td></tr><tr><td>• Bisection</td></tr><tr><td>• Newton-Raphson method</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Numerical computation" as one blob → ✅ split into: numerical computation, roots of algebraic, transcendental equations.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ answer with no steps → ✅ show each step and box the result.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ 1st law (inertia): a body stays in uniform motion unless acted on by a net external force..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Numerical computation: roots of algebraic and transcenden… (Computational Methods or Mechanics, Mathematics, class-11-notes) covers Numerical computation, roots of algebraic, transcendental equations. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Mechanics â€” Statics and Dynamics</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Application of Mechanics â€” Statics and Dynamics</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Mechanics â€” Statics and Dynamics to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Mechanics â€” Statics and Dynamics covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Numerical integration: Trapezoidal rule and Simpson's rule</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Numerical integration: Trapezoidal rule and Simpson's rule — Computational Methods or Mechanics, Mathematics (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: numerical integration, trapezoidal rule, simpson's rule · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** ∫xⁿ dx = x^{n+1}/(n+1) + C (n ≠ −1); ∫dx/x = ln|x| + C. Indefinite = family of antiderivatives.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** Integration by parts: ∫u dv = uv − ∫v du (LIATE choice). Substitution: let t = g(x), adjust dx.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** Definite integral = accumulation; area under y=f over [a,b] when f ≥ 0; area between curves = ∫|f−g| dx.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Fact.** Fundamental theorem: d/dx ∫_a^x f(t)dt = f(x); ∫_a^b f = F(b) − F(a).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Trapezoidal rule.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Simpson's rule.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Numerical integration</td></tr><tr><td>• Trapezoidal rule</td></tr><tr><td>• Simpson's rule</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Numerical integration" as one blob → ✅ split into: numerical integration, trapezoidal rule, simpson's rule.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ answer with no steps → ✅ show each step and box the result.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ ∫xⁿ dx = x^{n+1}/(n+1) + c (n ≠ −1); ∫dx/x = ln|x| + c. indefinite = family of antiderivatives..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Numerical integration: Trapezoidal rule and Simpson's rule (Computational Methods or Mechanics, Mathematics, class-11-notes) covers Numerical integration, Trapezoidal rule, Simpson's rule. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Mechanics (optional): Statics — forces and resultant forc…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Mechanics (optional): Statics — forces and resultant forc… — Computational Methods or Mechanics, Mathematics (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define 